---
// src/pages/graphic-engine.astro
// Technical breakdown of the XEMA custom C++ engine.
// Every claim here comes from the author's own description of the engine —
// don't add technical detail that he hasn't stated.
//
// ORDER: visuals first (PBR render, RenderDoc frame, WFC video), then the
// architecture and platform notes, so the page doesn't open on a stats panel.

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="XEMA engine | Fernando Fernández Andueza"
  description="XEMA, a C++ and OpenGL engine with ECS, deferred PBR rendering, Lua scripting and PhysX, for PC and Nintendo Switch (tested on an emulator)."
>
  <div class="container page-container">

    <!-- ── PAGE HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-header">
      <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} class="back-link">Back to portfolio</a>

      <h1>XEMA</h1>
      <p class="ed-lede">
        XEMA is an engine I built entirely myself in C++ and OpenGL, for PC and
        Nintendo Switch (tested only on an emulator). I'm currently working on a PS4 port using
        the console's own graphics API.
      </p>

      <p class="home-meta page-meta">University project. C++, OpenGL</p>

      <figure class="tech-figure tech-figure--hero">
        <img
          src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/pbr-still-life.jpg`}
          alt="A marble bust, a brass vase and a katana on a dark floor, lit from the side and casting shadows"
          width="1500"
          height="860"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Poly Haven models (marble bust, brass vase, katana) rendered with XEMA's deferred PBR
          pipeline and shadow mapping.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ── RENDERING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Rendering</h2>

      <p class="ed-prose">
        The renderer is deferred, with PBR, shadow mapping and instancing. This is one
        frame captured in RenderDoc.
      </p>

      <figure class="tech-figure">
        <img
          src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-final.png`}
          alt="Final image of a XEMA frame: a procedurally generated landscape with trees and houses"
          width="590"
          height="186"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <div class="gbuffer-grid">
          <div class="gbuffer-item">
            <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-albedo.png`} alt="Albedo render target" width="365" height="115" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="gbuffer-label">Albedo</span>
          </div>
          <div class="gbuffer-item">
            <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-normal.png`} alt="Normals render target" width="664" height="210" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="gbuffer-label">Normals</span>
          </div>
          <div class="gbuffer-item">
            <img src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-position.png`} alt="Position render target" width="563" height="177" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="gbuffer-label">Position</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          The geometry pass writes albedo, normals and position to three render targets, and
          the lighting pass reads them together with the shadow map to draw the final image.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="tech-figure tech-figure--natural">
        {/* Unreadable at phone width: tapping opens the full-size file */}
        <a
          href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-events.png`}
          class="figure-zoom"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          <img
            src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/renderdoc-events.png`}
            alt="RenderDoc event list with several glDrawElementsInstanced calls followed by the depth-only and colour passes"
            width="623"
            height="252"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </a>
        <figcaption>
          Draw calls from the same frame: repeated meshes are drawn
          with <code>glDrawElementsInstanced</code>, up to 107 instances in a single call.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PROCEDURAL GENERATION ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Procedural generation</h2>

      <p class="ed-prose">
        I implemented Wave Function Collapse in the engine. The landscape in the frame above
        comes from it.
      </p>

      <figure class="tech-figure">
        <div class="video-wrapper">
          <iframe
            src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/grZnYOMaqTQ?si=gf-zIuqm6VRQ5O4K"
            title="XEMA Wave Function Collapse demo"
            allow="encrypted-media; picture-in-picture"
            loading="lazy"
            allowfullscreen></iframe>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Wave Function Collapse demo generating nine different grids (four main ones and five
          transition grids) across multiple threads.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- ── ARCHITECTURE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Architecture</h2>

      <div class="ed-row">
        <div class="ed-body">
          <p>
            XEMA is built on an ECS architecture, with a JobSystem for work such as asynchronous
            mesh loading. It has Lua scripting and uses PhysX for physics. GLFW handles the
            window and input, and ImGui draws the debug panels for entities, components and
            performance.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ed-media ed-media--natural">
          <figure class="tech-figure">
            <img
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/project-images/xema/imgui-stats.png`}
              alt="ImGui stats panel showing FPS, frame time and the time spent in each engine section"
              width="290"
              height="281"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption>ImGui performance panel: frame time and time per engine section.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PLATFORMS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="tech-section">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Platforms</h2>

      <p class="ed-prose">
        The platform code is chosen at compile time: each build compiles only its own platform's
        source files, since a build only ever runs on one platform. I first designed it
        with interfaces, but dropped them because they weren't needed and every call had to go
        through a vtable for nothing.
      </p>

      <div class="ed-list" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <div class="ed-item">
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>Nintendo Switch: tested on an emulator</h3>
            <p>
              I ported it through <strong>libnx</strong> and <strong>EGL</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="ed-item">
          <div class="ed-item-body">
            <h3>PlayStation 4: in progress</h3>
            <p>
              Running on a devkit: the ECS and rendering of simple geometry. Still missing:
              mesh loading, textures, instancing, lights, shadows and PBR.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── PAGE NAV ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <nav class="page-nav" aria-label="More pages">
      <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} class="back-link">Back to portfolio</a>
    </nav>

  </div><!-- /.container -->

</Layout>
